  "step_1": "The decision reason is based on skewness, specifically preferring the lottery that is more right-skewed. A right-skewed lottery assigns a higher probability to unfavorable (i.e., worse) outcomes and a lower probability to favorable ones. In this context, 'unfavorable' outcomes are more negative (larger losses), and 'favorable' outcomes are less negative or zero. Lottery A has an 80% chance of -4000 (a large loss) and 20% chance of 0 (no loss). Lottery B has a 100% chance of -3000 (a certain, moderate loss). Lottery A is more right-skewed because it concentrates most of the probability (80%) on the worst outcome (-4000), while offering a small chance (20%) of avoiding loss entirely. Lottery B is symmetric (only one outcome) and thus not skewed. Therefore, according to the decision reason, Lottery A should be preferred because it is more right-skewed. The decision reason can be applied to this problem, and it would lead to choosing Lottery A.",
  "step_2": "The individual reports having looked at the amounts and probabilities and chose the lottery that allows the possibility of incurring no loss. They explicitly mention that the difference between a 3000 and 4000 Euro loss is not very significant to them. This indicates that the individual focused on the presence of a zero outcome in Lottery A (a chance to avoid loss entirely), which is absent in Lottery B. Their justification centers on the existence of a 'no loss' possibility and the relatively minor perceived difference between the two loss amounts. The deliberation emphasizes outcome possibilities (especially avoiding loss) rather than distributional shape, probability weighting, or skewness per se.",
  "step_3": "The decision reason focuses on skewness, particularly favoring more right-skewed distributions, which in this case is Lottery A. The individual also chose Lottery A, but for a different reason: the chance to avoid any loss (i.e., the 20% chance of 0 Euros). While both the reason and the individual prefer Lottery A, the basis for the preference differs. The decision reason relies on the overall shape of the distribution (high probability on worst outcome), whereas the individual is drawn to the favorable tail (non-zero chance of no loss). The individual does not mention or imply consideration of skewness, probability weighting over bad outcomes, or distributional asymmetry. Instead, they focus on the positive possibility of zero loss and downplay the magnitude difference between losses. Therefore, although the choice aligns with the decision reason, the underlying rationale does not match. There is no evidence the individual assessed skewness as defined.",
  "final_assessment_score": 20
