  "step_2": "The individual reports: 'Ich habe mir alle Beiträge und Wahrscheinlichkeiten angeschaut und die Lotterie gewählt, bei der es wahrscheinlicher ist kein Verlust zu erzielen' (I looked at all the amounts and probabilities and chose the lottery where it is more likely to incur no loss). This indicates that the individual focused on the probability of avoiding a loss (i.e., getting 0 Euros). They explicitly compare the likelihood of the favorable outcome (no loss) and choose based on which lottery offers a higher chance of this. The justification centers on maximizing the probability of the best possible outcome (0 loss), which reflects a preference for safety or a higher chance of avoiding any loss. The individual does not mention skewness, distribution shape, or comparative evaluation of unfavorable vs. favorable probabilities in structural terms.",
  "step_3": "The decision reason requires evaluating and comparing the skewness of the lotteries, specifically favoring the one with higher probability on unfavorable outcomes (more right-skewed). This would imply choosing A (25% loss chance) over B (20% loss chance). However, the individual’s reasoning focuses on maximizing the chance of no loss, which corresponds to choosing the lottery with the higher probability of 0 (i.e., 80% in B vs. 75% in A), so they would prefer B. The individual's justification is based on the likelihood of the favorable outcome, which is the opposite focus of the skewness reason (which emphasizes the weight on unfavorable outcomes). While both consider outcome probabilities, the skewness reason emphasizes the concentration of risk (unfavorable events), whereas the individual emphasizes the concentration of safety (favorable events). There is no indication that the individual assessed the distribution shape or considered what constitutes 'more right-skewed' in terms of probability mass on worse outcomes. The reasoning patterns are conceptually distinct. Therefore, it is unlikely that the individual used the skewness-based decision reason.",
  "final_assessment_score": 10
