  "step_2": "The individual reports: 'Ich habe mir alle Beiträge und Wahrscheinlichkeiten angeschaut und die Lotterie gewählt, bei der es wahrscheinlicher ist kein Verlust zu erzielen' (I looked at all the amounts and probabilities and chose the lottery where it is more likely to incur no loss). This indicates the individual focused on the probability of avoiding a loss (i.e., getting 0 Euros). Lottery A offers a 55% chance of no loss, while Lottery B offers only a 10% chance. The individual's justification centers on maximizing the chance of avoiding a loss. There is no mention of skewness, distribution shape, or comparison of how probabilities are distributed across unfavorable vs. favorable outcomes beyond the likelihood of zero loss. The deliberation is outcome-focused on the best possible result (no loss) and its probability.",
  "step_3": "The decision reason requires evaluating the overall skewness of the distribution—specifically, whether a greater portion of the probability mass is on unfavorable outcomes—and preferring the more right-skewed lottery (Lottery B in this case). However, the individual instead focuses narrowly on the probability of the best outcome (no loss), which is higher in Lottery A (55% vs. 10%). The information considered by the individual (chance of no loss) is not the same as the distributional asymmetry (skewness) that the reason requires. Furthermore, the justification—choosing based on higher chance of no loss—aligns with a 'maximize chance of best outcome' rule, not a skewness-based rule. There is no indication of consideration of how unfavorable outcomes are relatively more probable in one lottery versus the other in a broader distributional sense. Therefore, the individual's reasoning contradicts the skewness reason, which would favor the lottery with more probability on unfavorable outcomes (B), while the individual is drawn to the one with more probability on the favorable outcome (A).",
